<h2>The accident and the prosecution</h2>
<p>The fire broke out late at night on 30 March 2024, when an Indraprastha Gas Limited pipeline ignited during shifting or maintenance work near a Burari petrol pump. Two workers engaged by the contractor suffered severe burns and died. The police registered FIR No. 220/2024 at PS Burari under provisions covering negligent conduct with respect to fire (Section 285 IPC), causing hurt by an act endangering life (Section 337) and causing death by negligence (Section 304A), and in due course chargesheeted three men spanning the project\'s chain of responsibility: the utility\'s assistant manager, the contractor\'s proprietor, and the third-party inspection engineer. By the time the High Court was moved under Section 528 of the BNSS — the successor to Section 482 CrPC — the trial court at Tis Hazari was hearing arguments on charge.</p>

<h2>The settlement trail</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>May and September 2024</strong> — initial settlements executed, but only with the contractor; the other two accused remained outside them.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>16 September 2026</strong> — a fresh comprehensive settlement executed with all three petitioners, covering both deceased workers\' families.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Compensation</strong> — Rs. 20,65,000 confirmed received by one family; Rs. 20,00,000 agreed for the other, completed by demand draft of Rs. 9,00,000 handed over at the settlement.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Before the Court</strong> — the heirs supported quashing and stated they did not wish to pursue the case.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Gian Singh–Parbatbhai framework</h2>
<p>The inherent power of the High Court to quash proceedings is not a power to compound; it is exercised to secure the ends of justice or prevent abuse of process. The governing distinction is between offences that wound society — murder, rape, dacoity, offences under special statutes protecting public interests — and disputes with a predominantly private or civil character, such as those arising from commercial transactions or matrimonial relationships, where the parties have genuinely resolved their differences. In the second category, the Supreme Court has recognised that forcing a trial after a voluntary settlement usually produces nothing but a remote possibility of conviction and a certain waste of judicial time.</p>
<p>Fatal-negligence cases sit uncomfortably between the categories, because a death is involved but intent is not. Justice Pushkarna\'s reasoning shows how courts navigate that tension: the fire was prima facie accidental; the offences alleged were negligence-based, not crimes of depravity; the only persons with a direct stake — the workers\' families — had been compensated in full and wanted no prosecution; and the trial\'s realistic prospect, with the victims\' side unwilling, was acquittal after years of process.</p>

<h2>What this means for industrial-accident FIRs</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weight in a quashing petition</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nature of the offence</td><td>Negligence-based (304A/337) weighs towards quashing; intentional or depraved conduct against it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completeness of settlement</td><td>All accused and all victims\' families must be covered; the early partial settlements here were insufficient until the 2026 deed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voluntariness</td><td>Heirs appeared and confirmed the settlement and receipt of compensation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stage of trial</td><td>Pre-charge stage favoured quashing; a settlement after conviction stands on a different footing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public interest</td><td>Courts may still refuse where the accident reveals systemic endangerment of the public</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>A note on the new code</h2>
<p>Petitions of this kind are now filed under Section 528 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, which preserves the High Court\'s inherent powers in the same terms as Section 482 CrPC. The offences in the FIR, registered before 1 July 2024, continued under the IPC; conduct after that date would be charged under the corresponding provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — negligent conduct with respect to fire, causing hurt by endangering life, and causing death by negligence under Section 106(1) BNS.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Quashing on settlement remains discretionary and fact-specific; the presence of full, voluntary compensation to every affected family was central to this outcome, and the costs direction underscores that the inherent jurisdiction is invoked, not claimed as of right.</p>
</div>
